  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Double Speech**\n\nI\'ve discovered something in `App.tsx`. When the application is in `ANALYZING` mode and the user has `VISUAL` disability preferences, I noticed it utilizes `window.speechSynthesis.speak(...)`. I\'m beginning to wonder if this is the root cause of the perceived \"double\" speech the user is reporting, given these specific conditions.\n\n\n**Analyzing Audio Playback**\n\nI\'m now focusing on `ResultView.tsx` and the `handlePlayAudio` function. I suspected the double speech was originating there. It appears the audio is stopped before new audio is played. Looking into whether or not an external audio source might be inadvertently triggered. I also began to suspect that if one part of the code is also using `window.speechSynthesis.speak(...)`, while audio playback is active, that this can cause the issue.\n\n\n**Confirming Concurrent Speech Sources**\n\nI\'ve got it! I need to ensure that `handlePlayAudio` cancels `window.speechSynthesis` if it\'s running. It\'s likely that a concurrent use of both speech sources is generating the \"double\" speech. I\'ve re-examined `ResultView.tsx` and the `useEffect` hook. I see that speech is played during `AppMode.HEAR_IMAGES`, so I\'ll be implementing a solution there.\n\n\n**Addressing Double Timeout Issues**\n\nI realize `ResultView`\'s `useEffect` hook with an empty dependency array runs twice in development strict mode. This leads to two simultaneous `setTimeout` calls within `autoPlay()`. Consequently, after 500ms, *two* timeouts fire almost simultaneously, potentially triggering `handlePlayAudio` twice, and potentially generating \"double\" speech. I\'m focusing on cleaning up the first timeout.\n\n\n**Refining Playback Logic**\n\nI\'m revisiting `handlePlayAudio` and realized a crucial flaw in my previous approach. The asynchronous nature of `setIsAudioLoading(true)` means that in the subsequent timeout, `isAudioLoading` might still be false, leading to *two* concurrent fetch requests and audio playbacks. React 18+ strict mode exacerbates this issue. I\'ll need to clear the timeout properly to prevent double triggers. Additionally, I must ensure `handlePlayAudio` cancels `window.speechSynthesis` to avoid overlapping audio and standard speech synthesis.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Autoplay Safeguards**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `useEffect` hook within `ResultView.tsx`. Specifically, I\'m integrating a boolean ref to track autoplay execution. My goal is to prevent the `autoPlay` function from triggering repeatedly. I think I will implement a check to ensure that the logic within it only runs once.\n\n\n**Implementing Autoplay Refinement**\n\nI\'m now integrating a `useRef` to prevent double execution of autoplay within `ResultView.tsx`. This addresses issues in React strict mode and concurrent rendering. I\'ve incorporated the `hasPlayedRef` ref to ensure autoplay logic runs only once. This should address the double-triggering of audio playback I\'ve observed in the `useEffect` hook.\n\n\n**Isolating Uncancelled Speech**\n\nI\'ve been looking at how `App.tsx` handles speech synthesis. I\'ve realized that the code, specifically from lines 30 to 74, uses `window.speechSynthesis.speak(...)` but doesn\'t cancel or manage the speech queue when the application mode changes. When the app transitions to `RESULT`, that previous speech *might* still be speaking or queued, potentially contributing to the perceived \"double\" speech. I\'ll need to add a `window.speechSynthesis.cancel()` call on unmount to prevent this overlap.\n\n\n**Refining Speech Synthesis Control**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring proper cleanup within `App.tsx`\'s `useEffect` hook. I realized the importance of canceling any ongoing speech synthesis using `window.speechSynthesis.cancel()` on unmount, given the potential overlap with the audio played in `ResultView.tsx`. This is critical, as the \"I am now analyzing...\" message from speech synthesis can collide with the \"Visual description:\" played by `handlePlayAudio`, causing the user to hear both at the same time. The clean up return in the effect, and the cancellation within `handlePlayAudio` are imperative.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "### View the remainder of App.tsx\n\nLet\'s view the remaining lines of `/App.tsx` to double-check search functionality, settings buttons, or other places where speech synthesis or other voice tasks are executed."
    thinking_duration {
